WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.199
The historical Uber case begins with rider and
driver applications polling for changing trip

00:00:05.199 --> 00:00:10.229
state. The slides report that polling dominated
gateway traffic and that startup requests

00:00:10.229 --> 00:00:15.033
competed on weak mobile networks. RAMEN
separates the decision to send an update from

00:00:15.033 --> 00:00:17.237
creating its content and delivering it.

00:00:17.237 --> 00:00:22.228
This division targets both operational
efficiency and the difficulty of migrating many

00:00:22.228 --> 00:00:23.272
existing features.

00:00:23.272 --> 00:00:28.586
Fireball reacts to domain events and determines
recipients and message types. The API

00:00:28.586 --> 00:00:34.337
gateway assembles payloads using domain
services, reusing logic associated with earlier
pull

00:00:34.337 --> 00:00:39.276
APIs. A delivery system then owns connections
and retries. These are case-study

00:00:39.276 --> 00:00:43.589
responsibilities, not mandatory product names
for every architecture.

00:00:43.772 --> 00:00:48.738
In a course system, enrolment events, timetable
composition and device delivery can be

00:00:48.738 --> 00:00:53.762
separated in the same way. Should the connection
server also decide every business rule

00:00:53.762 --> 00:00:59.074
about when a ride update is needed? Separating
that decision reduces coupling between domain

00:00:59.074 --> 00:01:01.268
evolution and delivery infrastructure.

00:01:01.272 --> 00:01:06.814
RAMEN attaches delivery policy to payloads:
priority determines which pending updates matter

00:01:06.814 --> 00:01:11.994
first, expiry limits how long an update remains
useful, and replacement can retain the

00:01:11.994 --> 00:01:17.053
latest state instead of every intermediate
version. The historical SSE protocol adds

00:01:17.053 --> 00:01:21.028
sequence numbers, reconnect cursors and separate
acknowledgements.

00:01:21.272 --> 00:01:26.217
These application mechanisms supply recovery
behavior that a persistent transport alone

00:01:26.217 --> 00:01:27.865
cannot guarantee to the user.

00:01:27.865 --> 00:01:33.364
A lost acknowledgement is ambiguous: the client
may have processed a message even though the

00:01:33.364 --> 00:01:38.564
server retries it. Consumers must therefore
tolerate duplicates. Expiry also bounds the

00:01:38.564 --> 00:01:43.346
delivery promise: an offline device cannot
receive a timely update indefinitely.

00:01:43.365 --> 00:01:48.692
Distinguish coalescing state snapshots from
deduplicating identical messages; replacing an

00:01:48.692 --> 00:01:54.137
old location with a new location is reasonable,
but dropping an independent payment event is

00:01:54.137 --> 00:01:59.524
different. If the client processes sequence 3
but its acknowledgement is lost, may sequence

00:01:59.524 --> 00:02:00.411
3 arrive again?

00:02:00.411 --> 00:02:06.430
Yes. Retry can produce duplicates; the client
needs safe repeat processing or message-ID

00:02:06.430 --> 00:02:07.045
tracking.
